// A `view` constructor's visibility is not the author's to choose. `public` on it would be a standing
// offer to build a borrow over any memory the caller can name — the forge the mint rule closes — so it is
// REJECTED rather than silently downgraded to private.
//
// Rejecting is what keeps the source honest: the default is already private, so a silent downgrade would
// leave a declaration reading `public` while meaning the opposite. The same reasoning already governs a
// `view` FIELD, which may not carry a written visibility either.
type view Span {
    UnsafePtr<int32> at; int32 count;
    public unsafe ctor over(UnsafePtr<int32> at, int32 count) { this.at = at; this.count = count; }
}
fn int32 main() { return 0; }
